    "turn_text": "Now, you know, one of my favorite questions to ask is about Democrats, but about anything is what does he or she mean? What is this person? It's not just what they said. It's what are they trying to say? And here you have Bernie Sanders, who is, is still this, uh, beloved figure in progressive circles. and i i he does at least have the the courage of his convictions to say what he wants to do on a policy level he wants to be free college free college he wants to free this free that somebody else pay for all this stuff all these things and bernie sanders gets away as a result i think with just spewing nonsense all the time a government that works for everyone What is now? You could say, Buck, that's just, that's just a slogan, but, but is it? That's not supposed to just be a thing that said to get people in a government that, well, maybe it is, I don't know, but a government works for everyone. How? How? You know, he says this and I would like to drill a little bit deeper into this. What is the government? And why do we think that the government is supposed to work for everybody? When we work for, do we mean, like, in your employee? Or do we mean that it's supposed to function properly? Because if if anyone thinks that the government is really that that government employees think of themselves as your your employees you the American people it's often not really the case they think that they are are providing a service and a role that We need them and we're lucky to have them They do not you know public servants have gotten a very grandiose view of themselves in recent decades I'm just telling you I used to be one I know I know I speak of what I know I but that the government also belongs to a few billionaires like like like what like how who this is just all this is all piffle this is not in any way intellectually serious stuff that he offers up And you know, the, the, the talking points about these issues, you know, people would say, well, Buck, what about Trump and make America great again into this? I say, okay. Trump was said he wanted to fight political correctness and he has and does. And it's pretty clear. That means, that means when people say you can't say that, he says, actually I can and I'm going to and you can't stop me. Trump said he wanted to build a wall. He's tried. He's fighting for that. He's pushed different things. He's tried to endaka, you know, but that was a real idea. Those are real things that he's trying to do that he has been doing. He wanted to deregulate so the economy would grow. He has done that masterfully. It's one of the best things about this administration, the deregulation. He was going to cut taxes. He did. Now that's pretty standard Republican fair, but he did cut taxes. He's going to repeal and place Obamacare. That didn't really happen as planned, but you know, they're making some headway on prescription drug coverage and there were real ideas behind that campaign. We're not going to fight stupid wars in the Middle East. We haven't fought a stupid war in the Middle East because of Trump yet, unlike his two predecessors. So there were real ideas, you know, with, with Bernie Sanders, I feel like a lot of what he says and a lot of what people get excited about, uh, you know, the real policy proposals he puts forward are never going to happen. And the things that he repeats all the time don't really mean anything. Here's what here's to give you a sense of what I'm talking about, you know, uh, free college for all that's never going to happen. You're never going to find a way to pay for it. It's not going to happen. single pairs also not going to happen these are things that just won't happen and those are the ideas he's putting forward and the more general statements that he makes i don't even know what he's trying to what he's trying to make happen in the country things like all the government's going to work for everybody it's not going to belong to the billionaires anymore",
